WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:06.548
After Bill Sikes and Toby Crackit had abandoned him, Oliver lay unconscious in the ditch until early morning.

2
00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:13.705
At last a low cry of pain broke the stillness. It was the day after the robbery and Oliver was awakening.

3
00:00:13.706 --> 00:00:21.443
His left arm, crudely bandaged in a shawl, hung heavy at his side. He was so weak that he could scarcely sit up.

4
00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:25.023
When he had done so, he looked feebly around for help.

5
00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:30.864
Trembling from cold and exhaustion, he made an effort to stand but fell back onto the ground.

6
00:00:30.865 --> 00:00:37.777
For a while he remained unconscious, but something within warned him that if he stayed there, he would surely die.

7
00:00:37.778 --> 00:00:46.015
So he struggled to his feet and stumbled forward. Staggering on, he crept over fences and through hedges until he came to a road.

8
00:00:46.016 --> 00:00:49.769
Here the rain began to fall so heavily that it roused him.

9
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:57.253
He looked up and saw a house not far away. Perhaps the occupants would have pity on him and take him in.

10
00:00:57.254 --> 00:01:04.674
As he drew nearer, he felt that he had seen the house before. But where? Suddenly he remembered.

11
00:01:04.675 --> 00:01:11.650
It was the very house they had attempted to rob! Oliver knew he must flee, but he could scarcely stand.

12
00:01:11.651 --> 00:01:18.991
He climbed the front steps, knocked faintly at the door, and, with his strength failing him, sank down against a porch pillar.

13
00:01:18.992 --> 00:01:27.491
At that moment the two male servants who had gone out in pursuit of Oliver were entertaining the cook and the housemaid with the story of the previous night's adventures.

14
00:01:27.492 --> 00:01:35.285
"It was about half-past two when I woke up," said Mr. Giles, the butler, sipping tea in the kitchen. "I heard a noise." "What

15
00:01:35.286 --> 00:01:38.301
kind of noise?" asked the cook. "A

16
00:01:38.302 --> 00:01:47.309
noise like somebody was forcing open a door or a window. I decided to wake up Brittles and save him from being murdered in his bed." Here

17
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:54.047
everyone looked at Brittles, who was still called the lad-of-all-work even though he was over thirty years old. "I

18
00:01:54.048 --> 00:02:01.039
tossed off my bedclothes," said Mr. Giles. "Then I got softly out of bed, put on a pair of—" "There

19
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.535
are ladies present, Mr. Giles," warned Brittles. "A

20
00:02:04.536 --> 00:02:10.686
pair of shoes ." Mr. Giles turned to him and laid great emphasis on the word.

21
00:02:10.687 --> 00:02:21.257
"Then I seized the loaded pistol that I always take upstairs at night. I walked on tiptoes to Brittles' room. 'Brittles,' I said when I woke him, 'don't be frightened.'"

22
00:02:21.258 --> 00:02:24.360
" Was he frightened?" asked the

23
00:02:24.361 --> 00:02:27.511
cook. "Not a bit," replied Mr.

24
00:02:27.512 --> 00:02:32.019
Giles. "I would have died at once if it had been me," observed the

25
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.844
housemaid. "You're a woman," said

26
00:02:34.845 --> 00:02:40.074
Brittles. "Brittles is right." Mr. Giles nodded his head

27
00:02:40.075 --> 00:02:47.495
approvingly. "Nothing else is to be expected from a woman. But being men, we took a lantern and groped our way

28
00:02:47.496 --> 00:02:57.566
downstairs." Mr. Giles had risen from his chair and taken two steps forward to demonstrate their actions when a sound startled everyone in the kitchen. The cook and the housemaid

29
00:02:57.567 --> 00:03:04.044
screamed. "It was a knock at the front door," said Mr. Giles, perfectly calm. "Open the door,

30
00:03:04.044 --> 00:03:05.972
somebody." Nobody

31
00:03:05.972 --> 00:03:12.027
moved. "A knock coming so early in the morning seems strange. But somebody must open the

32
00:03:12.027 --> 00:03:15.154
door." Mr. Giles looked at Brittles, who said

33
00:03:15.154 --> 00:03:21.788
nothing. "If Brittles would prefer to open the door in the presence of witnesses, I will be one," said Mr.

34
00:03:21.789 --> 00:03:27.986
Giles. Brittles went upstairs, followed by Mr. Giles and the two women, who were too frightened to stay behind in the

35
00:03:27.987 --> 00:03:32.804
kitchen. They talked loudly to warn any evil person outside that they were strong in

36
00:03:32.805 --> 00:03:39.796
number. Brittles opened the door while the others peeked over his shoulders. And there sat poor little Oliver

37
00:03:39.797 --> 00:03:46.955
Twist. "A boy!" exclaimed Mr. Giles, pushing Brittles aside. "Why, Brittles, do you know who this

38
00:03:46.956 --> 00:03:50.796
is?" Mr. Giles grabbed Oliver and lugged him into the front

39
00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:58.764
hall. "Here's one of the thieves, ma'am!" Mr. Giles called up the staircase. "He's wounded, miss! I shot him and Brittles held the

40
00:03:58.765 --> 00:04:06.526
light." The two female servants ran upstairs to carry the news to old Mrs. Maylie, the owner of the house, and her niece,

41
00:04:06.527 --> 00:04:11.526
Rose. Mr. Giles tried to revive Oliver so that he wouldn't die before he could be

42
00:04:11.527 --> 00:04:16.835
hanged. In the midst of all this commotion, a sweet female voice quieted everything in an

43
00:04:16.836 --> 00:04:20.597
instant. "Giles!" whispered Rose, a pretty young

44
00:04:20.598 --> 00:04:27.486
woman. "Don't be frightened, miss. He didn't make a very desperate resistance. I was soon too much for

45
00:04:27.487 --> 00:04:35.819
him." "Hush!" replied the young lady. "You frighten my aunt as much as the thieves did. Is the poor creature badly

46
00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.914
hurt?" "Wounded

47
00:04:37.915 --> 00:04:44.065
desperately." "He looks as if he is dying, miss," called Brittles. "Would you like to come and look at him in case he

48
00:04:44.066 --> 00:04:54.630
does?" Rose preferred to consult with her aunt. She soon returned to the top of the stairs with the instructions that the wounded person was to be carried carefully up to Mr. Giles' room.

49
00:04:54.631 --> 00:04:59.226
Brittles was ordered to ride to Chertsey and send for the doctor and a policeman.

50
00:04:59.227 --> 00:05:07.377
"Poor fellow!" said Rose as she refused yet another offer to look at the thief. "Oh, treat him kindly, Giles, for my sake."

51
00:05:07.378 --> 00:05:13.035
An hour later Dr. Losberne bustled into the room where Rose and her aunt were finishing breakfast.

52
00:05:13.036 --> 00:05:21.607
"My dear Mrs. Maylie! I never heard of such a thing! Breaking into a house in the middle of the night!" exclaimed the doctor.

53
00:05:21.608 --> 00:05:25.877
"You ought to be dead with fright! And you, Miss Rose—"

54
00:05:25.878 --> 00:05:33.535
"Yes, indeed," said Rose, interrupting him. "But there is a poor creature upstairs whom my aunt wishes you to see."

55
00:05:33.536 --> 00:05:40.020
"Ah, to be sure. Lead me to him, Giles. This was your handiwork, I understand."

56
00:05:40.021 --> 00:05:43.488
The doctor followed the butler upstairs.

57
00:05:43.489 --> 00:05:48.425
The doctor was absent much longer than either he or the ladies had anticipated.

58
00:05:48.426 --> 00:05:55.671
The servants ran up and down the stairs as a large box was fetched out of his carriage and the bedroom bell was rung very often.

59
00:05:55.672 --> 00:05:59.425
At last Dr. Losberne rejoined Rose and Mrs. Maylie.

60
00:05:59.426 --> 00:06:04.742
"This is a very extraordinary thing," he said. "Have you seen the thief?"

61
00:06:04.743 --> 00:06:07.488
"No," said Mrs. Maylie.

62
00:06:07.489 --> 00:06:17.496
"Then you should come with me at once." He offered one arm to Rose and held out his other hand to Mrs. Maylie. "I promise there's nothing alarming in his appearance."

63
00:06:17.497 --> 00:06:23.821
Leading them upstairs, he paused for a moment outside the bedroom. Then he threw open the door.

64
00:06:23.822 --> 00:06:27.067
"Oh, my!" said Mrs. Maylie.

65
00:06:27.068 --> 00:06:31.437
"He's only a boy!" gasped Rose.

